<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6933"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lubbock<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lubbock<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 10, 2005 - 4:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Nm_pi: <BR> <BR>Okay, I&#39;ll admit to being a conspiracy nut. I believe the CIA was involved at ground &#40;or grassy knoll&#41; level in the J.F.K. assassination. I also believe Sirhan Sirhan was brainwashed by the CIA, possibly as a guinea pig under austensibly discontinued Project ULTRA, when he shot Robert Kennedy. <BR> <BR>That being said, innuendo and facts about that group of spooks, with their secret-decoder-ring, &#34;I could tell ya but then I&#39;d have to kill ya&#34; mind sets could fuel a library full of books. <BR> <BR>William Casey, former Director of Central Intelligence admitted in a book ghostwritten by Bob Woodward that the CIA capitulated in the importation of cocaine into the U.S.  <BR> <BR>John Kerry chaired a Senate sub-committee hearing on similar alegations in 1987. Their findings - best case: internal memoranda making it mandatory to report evidence of drug trafficking to the F.B.I., Customs and the D.E.A. were issued with a nod and a wink among the ivory tower bean-counters, and never reached operatives in the field. A more nefarious view of the evidence collected suggests that the CIA &#34;reccomended&#34; air transport companies to Secretary of State Duerling, who were subsequently contracted for hauling &#34;humanitarian aide&#34; to Contras operating out of Nicaragua, and that the CIA actively facilitated safe transport of the drug laden planes on their return trips by persuading Coast Guard and Customs personnel to be away from their radar screens at a given time, or by setting up LORAN beacons that the smugglers honed in on, allowing them to fly in under the radar. <BR> <BR>In the words of System of a Down, &#34;Prison Song&#34; on the &#34;Toxicity&#34; CD: <BR>&#34;Utilizing drugs to pay for secret wars around the world. Drugs are now your global policy. Now you police the globe.&#34; And: &#34;Drug money is used to rig elections and train brutal corporate sponsored dictators around the world.&#34;  <BR> <BR>If you need more reason to consider that these type of people are capable of anything, rent Oliver Stone&#39;s &#34;J.F.K.&#34; Better yet, read the Warren report - the explaination of the &#34;Magic bullet&#34; is too ludicrous to be laughable. <BR> <BR>Where am I going with all this? <BR> <BR>In the &#34;possible victims&#34; thread Superbike7 wrote of Jacob Allen, who had been bound to railroad tracks near Milan, Kansas and was hit by a train around 3am on 7/5/04. Binding one&#39;s self with bailing wire must be quite a trick. LE speculated suicide. Jacob was valedictorian of his high school and a multi-sport star. He also reportedly had detailed long-term life goals - not your standard profile of a suicidal teen.  <BR> <BR>In another post, Teardrop noted that if the victim were given a paralitic agent - available through a veterinary supply outlet - he might be alert but unable to move as the train approached. Sounds like a sadists wet dream. <BR> <BR>7 also mentioned that he&#39;d come across mention of a similar double murder near Mena, Arkansas. Mena and its airport are/were a notorious hotbed for the CIA and drug smuggling activity. <BR> <BR>Didn&#39;t Joseph Otero serve some of his military time in South America. I wonder if his service records have been classified or deleted? <BR> <BR>In the &#34;BTK only&#34; thread, &#34;BTK&#34; posted, on 2/6/05 at 10:23pm: &#34;fbi...wpd...CIA are a grave joke.&#34; CIA was the only one capitalized. Also, the CIA is not supposed conduct domestic operations. <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t believe that BTK is a spy gone rogue, but if you jam the data together, fudge, and squint a bit it almost seems a possibility.   <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6933-->
